 "standard_text": "2A.5B formulate exponential and logarithmic equations that model real-world situations, including exponential relationships written in recursive notation; 2A.5D solve exponential equations of the form y = ab^x where a is a nonzero real number and b is greater than zero and not equal to one and single logarithmic equations having real solutions; 2A.5E determine the reasonableness of a solution to a logarithmic equation",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra II worksheets for Texas TEKS 2A.5B, 2A.5D and 2A.5E: solve exponential and log equations, extraneous solutions, applications and recursive rules. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Rewrite with a common base, or take a log of both sides.",
 "key_sub": "Check every answer: log inputs must be positive.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Common base: 8<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 32 → 2<sup>3<i>x</i></sup> = 2<sup>5</sup> → 3<i>x</i> = 5.</li><li>Otherwise take a log of both sides: 5<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 40 → <i>x</i> = ln 40 ÷ ln 5.</li><li>log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>A</i> = <i>c</i> → <i>A</i> = <i>b</i><sup><i>c</i></sup>.</li><li><b>Check</b> every answer: a log input must be positive. A value that makes it 0 or negative is extraneous.</li><li>Recursive rule: <i>a</i><sub><i>n</i></sub> = <i>r</i> · <i>a</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>; explicit rule: <i>a</i><sub><i>n</i></sub> = <i>a</i><sub>0</sub> · <i>r</i><sup><i>n</i></sup>.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>log<sub>2</sub> <i>x</i> + log<sub>2</sub>(<i>x</i> − 2) = 3</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i>(<i>x</i> − 2) = 8</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> = 4 or <i>x</i> = −2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−2 makes log<sub>2</sub>(−2) undefined</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Keeping an extraneous solution</td><td>Check that every log input is positive.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding instead of multiplying</td><td>Doubling: <i>a</i><sub><i>n</i></sub> = 2 · <i>a</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>.</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplying the exponent by the base</td><td>2<sup>3</sup> = 8, not 6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
